    "['pseudowire-class [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols l2circuit pseudowire-type ethernet', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set protocols l2circuit pseudowire-type ethernet', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent for the Cisco `pseudowire-class` configuration:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols l2circuit pseudowire-type ethernet\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, there isn't a direct 1:1 command for `pseudowire-class` since Juniper handles L2VPN pseudowires differently. The above command sets the pseudowire type to Ethernet (equivalent to Cisco's default encapsulation). Juniper automatically uses LDP as the signaling protocol for L2VPN unless specifically configured otherwise.\\n\\nFor a more complete translation, you would typically configure the L2VPN settings under `protocols l2circuit` in Juniper, specifying neighbor and interface parameters differently than in Cisco.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols l2circuit pseudowire-type ethernet'}]]": 1,
    "['encapsulation [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'encapsulation mpls-over-udp;', 'parent_command': ['set protocols l2circuit pseudowire-type ethernet'], 'root': 'set protocols l2circuit pseudowire-type ethernet', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Juniper equivalent command for `encapsulation mpls`:\\n\\n##\\nencapsulation mpls-over-udp;\\n##\\n\\nNote: Juniper typically uses `mpls-over-udp` as the encapsulation type for pseudowires, which is functionally similar to Cisco's basic MPLS encapsulation in this context. The exact implementation might vary slightly depending on the specific Juniper platform and use case.\", 'trans_template': 'encapsulation mpls-over-udp;'}]]": 1,
    "['l2vpn vfi context [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set routing-instances LDP1 instance-type vpls', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set routing-instances LDP1 instance-type vpls', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `l2vpn vfi context LDP1` command:\\n\\n##\\nset routing-instances LDP1 instance-type vpls\\nset routing-instances LDP1 vlan-id 1\\n##\\n\\nNote: The Juniper configuration requires additional parameters to fully match the Cisco functionality. The `vpn id 1` from Cisco is translated to `vlan-id 1` in Juniper, and the instance type is set to VPLS (Virtual Private LAN Service), which is Juniper's equivalent to Cisco's VFI (Virtual Forwarding Instance) for L2VPN services.\", 'trans_template': 'set routing-instances LDP1 instance-type vpls'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set routing-instances LDP1 vlan-id 1', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set routing-instances LDP1 vlan-id 1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `l2vpn vfi context LDP1` command:\\n\\n##\\nset routing-instances LDP1 instance-type vpls\\nset routing-instances LDP1 vlan-id 1\\n##\\n\\nNote: The Juniper configuration requires additional parameters to fully match the Cisco functionality. The `vpn id 1` from Cisco is translated to `vlan-id 1` in Juniper, and the instance type is set to VPLS (Virtual Private LAN Service), which is Juniper's equivalent to Cisco's VFI (Virtual Forwarding Instance) for L2VPN services.\", 'trans_template': 'set routing-instances LDP1 vlan-id 1'}]]": 1,
    "['vpn id [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'vpn-id 1;', 'parent_command': ['set routing-instances LDP1 instance-type vpls'], 'root': 'set routing-instances LDP1 instance-type vpls', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `vpn id 1` command:\\n\\n##\\nvpn-id 1;\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper's MX series routers for L2VPN configuration, the equivalent command is `vpn-id` followed by the VPN identifier value, with a semicolon at the end (Juniper's standard syntax). The command would typically appear under the `bridge-domains` or `routing-instances` hierarchy in Junos configuration.\", 'trans_template': 'vpn-id 1;'}]]": 1,
